00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
in the past few videos we've talked  about how different recommenders can  help with cost performance and security  in your google cloud projects  for enterprises and larger organizations  it's likely that you'll have many  projects  in order to take full advantage of the  benefits from recommendations you might  need to scale how you handle them such  as incorporating them into a pipeline to  programmatically manage them  today let's go over some tips for how an  enterprise could leverage  recommendations at scale and show you  how to export your recommendations to  bigquery so that they can be reviewed  across your organization  the benefits of active assist work great  even if you only have one project but  they really begin to shine in an  organization with lots of projects  in each project various recommenders  will help you apply the principle of  least privilege for security minimize  resource waste from workloads and  maximize the performance of business  critical applications  because of the increased scale of  operations in a large organization  you'll want to review and take action on  recommendations with minimal  administrative work and time  what this looks like for your  organization will be unique to your  workloads infrastructure security and  processes  while the exact details depend on what  works best for you we recommend a  three-phased approach for adopting  recommenders at scale  as you move through these phases at your  own pace the potential for automation  increases  let's start with the first phase  accessing recommendations through the  recommendation hub in the google cloud  console  the recommendation hub is the simplest  way of accessing and managing  recommendations in your projects and  organization  reviewing recommendations in the  recommendation hub will familiarize you  with different recommenders available so  that you can think about how you might  like to prioritize them when building a  pipeline in a later phase  working from the console also includes  being able to set up a bigquery data  transfer to export your recommendations  to a bigquery data set  when you do this two tables will be  generated recommender export where the  recommendations are exported and insight  export where the insights that help you  focus on important patterns are exported  insights can provide additional context  to recommendations or they can be used  independently  by exporting recommendations to bigquery  you're able to look at details for  project level recommendations across  your organization  with that ability you can expand by  creating dashboards to help you  visualize and report the potential  impact of applying recommendations  once you're familiarized with the  console and with the data exported to  bigquery the next phase of recommenders  at scales is to use the apis  all of the recommendation data that you  can access through the recommender hub  or the bigquery export is also available  via an api  the recommendation resource returned by  the api will include lots of information  about a single recommendation including  the priority the expected impact and the  status of the recommendation such as  active or dismissed  you can also use an api to return  insights just like in the bigquery  exports insights can provide additional  context for associated recommendations  for example a recommendation from the  iim policy recommender to reduce the  permissions for a user  may have an associated insight that  includes the permissions that have been  used in the past 90 days and therefore  should not be removed  you can use the api to view  recommendations and insights as well as  to update their status if you choose to  take action on them  when getting started with the api you'll  probably want to require manual review  but you can start introducing automation  to help scale and handle more  recommendations this can also help lay  the foundation for the third phase  integrating recommendations into your  organization's devops pipelines  if your organization has a  well-established devops strategy changes  to your cloud infrastructure might be  managed through continuous integration  and continuous deployment processes  the goal for scaling recommendations  should be to automate the review  analysis  and implementation  for example you could set up cloud run  to regularly retrieve recommendations  via the api and then automatically  generate a pull request in your  infrastructure as code repository for  that recommended change  that pull request can be reviewed by an  administrative team and if accepted and  merged the included recommendation can  move on in the pipeline and subsequently  be applied to your cloud infrastructure  by incorporating recommendations into  your ci cd pipeline you can proactively  monitor recommendations  apply changes that are kept consistent  in code can easily be reviewed tested  and rolled back using the same processes  used for the rest of your infrastructure  this level of automation and integration  is ideal for large organizations and  enterprises but no matter what scale  you're working with you can still always  take advantage of recommendations  it can also be helpful to start slow and  take the time to get familiar with  recommendations and how they impact your  cloud infrastructure before you  implement a full-blown automation  process  we've taken a look at the recommendation  hub before so now let's take a look at  how we can get those recommendations  into bigquery  you can do this by setting up a bigquery  data transfer with the recommendations  as the source  to do that you'll need to have the  recommendations exporter role assigned  on your organization's iim policy this  will give you access to the  organization's recommendations across  projects  start in the recommendation hub where  you can find the export button at the  top of the page  the first step is to provide the  destination project where the  recommendation data will be stored  the destination project can be any  project that you have access to but  you'll need to have the following  permissions for that project  bigquery data sets create bigquery  transfers get bigquery transfers update  resource manager projects update service  usage services enable and service usage  services git  in addition the bigquery api and  bigquery transfer api will need to be  enabled for that project  the next step is to configure the  transfer with a few details like name  schedule and a target bigquery data set  when it comes to choosing a schedule  you'll have the option of daily weekly  monthly or your own custom schedule  you'll also be able to configure  notifications via email if the transfer  fails or publish to a pub sub topic when  a transfer completes  once complete you'll be able to see your  new bigquery data transfer details in  the console and it should begin  populating data within about two days  once the transfer has had a chance to  run you'll see those two tables we  mentioned earlier in your selected  bigquery data set recommendation export  and insight export we'll include a link  to the documentation where you can see  the full table schema recommendations  will also have a field for associated  insights so you can look up any insight  details in the insight exports table  with recommendation data exported to  bigquery you'll be able to write custom  queries to analyze recommendations from  there you can expand by exporting to  data studio or google sheets where you  can further analyze for patterns and  opportunities to optimize  you can also begin to automate by  accessing that recommender api and  follow that up by including  recommendations into programmatic  processes as part of your established ci  cd pipelines  recommendations are available for all  google cloud users if your organization  has a support package you'll also have  access to the full api and bigquery  export we'll include a link in the  description with more information  we'll also include some links in the  description if you'd like to find even  more information and guidance on using  active assisted scale  and remember  the right solution depends on your  organization and what makes sense for  you don't be afraid to implement these  over time instead of jumping immediately  to the most complicated solution  stay tuned for more videos and thanks  for watching

